[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Generics, Collections, and Functional Operators in Flutter"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dart, the foundation of Flutter, provides powerful tools for managing data structures efficiently. Understanding generics, collections (Lists, Sets, Maps), and functional operators (map, where, reduce) is essential for writing clean, scalable, and type-safe Flutter applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Generics in Dart"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics allow you to write reusable code by parameterizing types. Instead of locking a class or method down to a specific data type like String or int, generics defer the type definition until instantiation."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class Cache<T> {
  final Map<String, T> _data = {};

  void set(String key, T value) {
    _data[key] = value;
  }

  T? get(String key) {
    return _data[key];
  }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the Cache class can hold any data type. When you initialize Cache<String>(), the compiler enforces type safety, preventing runtime errors."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Working with Collections"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dart features three primary core collection types: Lists (ordered groups of objects), Sets (collections of unique items), and Maps (key-value pairs). In Flutter development, these are heavily used for managing state and rendering UI lists."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"List<String> categories = ['Technology', 'Finance', 'Health'];
Set<int> uniqueIds = {101, 102, 103};
Map<String, dynamic> user = {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 30};"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Functional Operators on Collections"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Functional programming operators like where, map, expand, and reduce allow you to transform and query collections declaratively without writing verbose for-loops."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where(): Filters items based on a boolean condition."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"map(): Transforms each element in the collection into a new form."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"fold() / reduce(): Combines collection elements into a single accumulated value."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"void processTransactions() {
  List<double> transactions = [150.0, -20.0, 300.0, -50.0];

  // Filter out negative transactions and sum them up
  double totalPositive = transactions
      .where((amount) => amount > 0)
      .fold(0.0, (sum, element) => sum + element);

  print('Total Positive: \$totalPositive');
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Practical Application in Flutter UI"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common pattern in Flutter is mapping a List of data models directly to a List of Widgets using the map operator combined with the spread operator (...)."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"class ProductListWidget extends StatelessWidget {
  final List<String> products;

  const ProductListWidget({Key? key, required this.products}) : super(key: key);

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Column(
      children: [
        ...products.map((product) => ListTile(
          title: Text(product),
        )).toList(),
      ],
    );
  }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining generics for robust architecture, collections for data structuring, and functional operators for data manipulation, you can write concise, highly performant, and maintainable code in your Flutter applications."}]
